/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An ideal gas mixture is a set of gases sharing one vessel at the same temperature T and pressure p, with no chemical reaction and no forces between molecules.
   - The mole fraction of component i is yi = ni/n.
   - A mixture behaves as one gas with molar mass Mmix = m/n = Σ yiMi and gas constant Rmix = Ru/Mmix, where Ru = 8.314 kJ/(kmol·K) is the universal gas constant.
   - Mmix = Σ yiMi Rmix = Ru/Mmix
   - yi = ni/n is the mole fraction, dimensionless; Mi is the molar mass of component i in kg/kmol; Mmix is the mixture molar mass in kg/kmol; Ru = 8.314 kJ/(kmol·K); Rmix is the mixture gas constant in kJ/(kg·K).
   - Dalton's model takes the mixture apart.
   - Divide pi by p = nRuT/V and the temperature and volume cancel: pi = yip.
   - The V and T in p = nRuT/V belong to the whole mixture, with Ru = 8.314 kJ/(kmol·K) giving p in kPa.
   - Amagat's model keeps the mixture pressure p and temperature T and asks a different question: what volume Vi would component i occupy alone at p and T?
   - The same mole fraction appears in both laws: pi/p = Vi/V = yi.
*/
