/* book Worked example: Q: A rigid tank of 2 m³ holds 0.8 kg of oxygen and 2.8 kg of nitrogen at 300 K. Take Ru = 8.314 kJ/(kmol·K), MO2 = 32 kg/kmol and MN2 = 28 kg/kmol. Find the mole fractions, the total and partial pressures, the partial volumes, the mixture molar mass and gas constant, and the entropy generated if the two gases were first held apart at the same T and p and then allowed to mix.
   1. Moles: nO2 = 0.8/32 = 0.025 kmol and nN2 = 2.8/28 = 0.100 kmol, so n = 0.125 kmol.
   2. Mole fractions: yO2 = 0.025/0.125 = 0.2 and yN2 = 0.100/0.125 = 0.8; check 0.2 + 0.8 = 1.00. (Mass fractions are different: wO2 = 0.8/3.6 = 0.222.)
   3. Total pressure: p = nRuT/V = 0.125 × 8.314 × 300/2 = 311.78/2 = 155.9 kPa.
   4. Dalton: pO2 = 0.2 × 155.9 = 31.2 kPa and pN2 = 0.8 × 155.9 = 124.7 kPa; 31.2 + 124.7 = 155.9 kPa.
   5. Amagat: VO2 = 0.2 × 2 = 0.4 m³ and VN2 = 0.8 × 2 = 1.6 m³; together 2 m³.
   6. Mixture: Mmix = 0.2 × 32 + 0.8 × 28 = 6.4 + 22.4 = 28.8 kg/kmol, and m = 0.125 × 28.8 = 3.6 kg, which matches 0.8 + 2.8. Rmix = 8.314/28.8 = 0.2887 kJ/(kg·K).
   7. Check: pV/m = 155.9 × 2/3.6 = 86.6 kJ/kg and RmixT = 0.2887 × 300 = 86.6 kJ/kg.
   8. Entropy of mixing: ΔS = −8.314 × (0.025 ln 0.2 + 0.100 ln 0.8) = −8.314 × (−0.04024 − 0.02231) = 8.314 × 0.06255 = 0.520 kJ/K.
   Answer: yO2 = 0.2, yN2 = 0.8; p = 155.9 kPa with pO2 = 31.2 kPa and pN2 = 124.7 kPa; VO2 = 0.4 m³, VN2 = 1.6 m³; Mmix = 28.8 kg/kmol, Rmix = 0.2887 kJ/(kg·K); ΔS of mixing = 0.520 kJ/K. */
